previously on AI adventures we looked at  the tensor processing unit v1 how it  came to be and its design it only seems  right that we now go back and check out  TPU b2 and b3 what changes and  improvements were made to create these  new state-of-the-art chips and how can  you use them to power your machine  learning stay tuned to find out  [Music]  [Music]  welcome to AI adventures where we  explore the art science and tools of  machine learning my name is Yu phango  and on this episode we're going to take  a look at the architecture of the TPU v2  and v3 as well as how the hardware  design choices come together to create a  powerful specialized machine learning  chip after the TPU v1 was created the  team took the lessons they learned and  applied it to designing the TPU v2 as  you can see it's considerably larger  than the first TPU and features four  chips instead of just one additionally  it's got some massive heat sinks it has  a hundred and eighty teraflops of  compute meaning that it can do 180  trillion floating-point operations per  second and it does both training and  prediction now the layout of the TPU v2  is quite interesting each board has  those four chips but each chip has two  cores each core then contains a matrix  unit a vector unit and a scaler unit all  connected to eight gigabytes of high  bandwidth memory that means in total  each board has eight cores and 64 gigs  of memory and the matrix unit is a 128  by 128 systolic array what's special  about the TPU v2 though is its use of a  new data type called the B float16 the B  float16 combines the range of a 32-bit  floating-point number with just the  storage space of only a 16-bit floating  point number and what does the B&amp;B  float16 stand for brain says it comes to  us from the Google brain  of course you can't get something for  nothing and what we lose is precision  the B float16 is able to represent far  fewer decimal places than a standard  16-bit floating point number however the  team determined that this was worth the  trade-off  since neural networks can operate at a  reduced precision while retaining  sufficiently high accuracy and using the  B float16 enabled the TPU v2 to fit in  much more computational power combined  with designing an integrated chip and  placing it on the board with three  compatriots so you have four chips per  board the TPU v2 achieves much higher  performance than the b1 now for the  really exciting part the TPU v2 is  arranged into pods one TPU v2 pod is 60  40 P use they're all connected together  and you can use an entire pod as if it  was one machine and since there are two  cores per chip with those four chips per  board and 64 TP use per pod that  multiplies out to 512 cores in a TPU pod  totaling up to over eleven petaflop s'  of processing power you can use smaller  subdivisions of the pods as well such as  say a quarter pod or a half pod what's  really extraordinary is when you start  using TPU pods to train up  state-of-the-art models on benchmark  data sets for example you can train  resin at 50 in 39 hours but thirty  minutes and that's only on a half pod  with just 32 TP use and when you look at  the training process used they're using  a batch size of 8000 images per batch  quite amazing and this is part of what  allows that training to proceed at such  a breakneck pace now I promised to talk  about TPU v3 as well so here it is as  you can see they took the v2 and they  made it blue but additionally of course  these chips use water cooling this  allows it to take up much less vertical  space so the TPU v3 pods can then  support many  warranty pues in it and a full TPU V 3  pod comes in at 8 times faster than a b2  pod and weighs in at over a hundred  petaflop s' of compute power today TP  use are available as individual boards  or as pod slices and both TPU v2 and TPU  v3 are available for you to use it only  costs a couple of dollars per hour  depending on which region used on which  TPU you choose pricing for TPU pods is  as you might imagine somewhat more  substantial with all those TPU is  connected together but for truly  large-scale training jobs that you need  to have run quickly  it is worth it programming the TPU is  just consistently getting easier and  easier with the recent release of  tensorflow 2.0 you can even use the  Charis api to code up your training for  cheap use there are great samples and  implementations of many state of the art  models so what you can do is just swap  in your custom data set and you're off  to the races you can learn more and get  started at Chico slash cloud TPU thanks  for watching this episode of cloud AI  adventures and if you enjoyed it click  that like button and be sure to  subscribe to get all the latest episodes  right when they come out for now check  out cloud abuse and see how quickly they  can train your models at scale  you  [Music]
 

